<p>Adapted from <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia</a>:</p>
<p><strong>Aristotle</strong> (384–322 BC) was an ancient Greek philosopher and scientist from Stagira in northern Greece. After his father Nicomachus died during his childhood, Proxenus of Atarneus raised him. At age 17 or 18, Aristotle entered Plato's Academy in Athens, where he studied for 20 years until around 347 BC.</p>
<p><strong>Breadth of Work</strong></p>
<p>Aristotle created the first comprehensive system of Western philosophy, writing on virtually every field of knowledge:</p>
<ul>
<li>Natural sciences: physics, biology, zoology</li>
<li>Philosophy: metaphysics, logic, ethics, aesthetics</li>
<li>Arts: poetry, theater, music</li>
<li>Communication: rhetoric, linguistics</li>
<li>Society: politics and government</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Career and Methods</strong></p>
<p>After Plato's death, Aristotle left Athens and became tutor to Alexander the Great at Philip II of Macedon's request (343 BC). This position provided him with extensive resources, allowing him to establish a library at the Lyceum and produce hundreds of books on papyrus scrolls.</p>
<p>Initially influenced by his teacher Plato's idealism, Aristotle later developed his own empirical approach, arguing that all knowledge comes from sensory perception and observation.</p>
<p><strong>Lasting Influence</strong></p>
<p>Aristotle's impact spans over two millennia:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Medieval Period</strong>: His scientific views dominated scholarship from Late Antiquity through the Renaissance, only being systematically replaced during the Enlightenment</li>
<li><strong>Scientific Contributions</strong>: Some observations, like his description of the octopus's reproductive arm, weren't confirmed until the 19th century</li>
<li><strong>Logic</strong>: His formal study of logic became the foundation for modern logical systems</li>
<li><strong>Religious Thought</strong>: His metaphysics profoundly influenced Jewish, Islamic, and Christian theology (Muslim scholars called him "The First Teacher")</li>
<li><strong>Modern Philosophy</strong>: His ethics experienced renewed interest with contemporary virtue ethics</li>
</ul>
<p>Though Cicero praised Aristotle's writing as "a river of gold," only about one-third of his original works survive today. Nevertheless, all aspects of his philosophy remain subjects of active academic study.</p>
